1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,439
Two disks, each prepared as a physical volume. But LVM cannot use them yet.

2
00:00:05,459 --> 00:00:10,364
First, they have to be put into a volume group: one shared pool of space.

3
00:00:10,834 --> 00:00:14,270
In this video: assign physical volumes to volume groups.

4
00:00:18,790 --> 00:00:21,373
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:21,843 --> 00:00:24,319
Assign physical volumes to volume groups.

6
00:00:24,789 --> 00:00:28,640
We'll start with two physical volumes, create a volume group with one,

7
00:00:28,660 --> 00:00:30,742
add the second, and take it out again.

8
00:00:40,262 --> 00:00:41,878
Remember the three layers.

9
00:00:41,898 --> 00:00:45,653
Physical volumes at the bottom, volume groups in the middle.

10
00:00:45,673 --> 00:00:51,442
A volume group takes one or more physical volumes and pools their space into one big bucket.

11
00:00:59,912 --> 00:01:02,903
Here, vdb and vdc are five gigabytes each.

12
00:01:02,923 --> 00:01:06,702
Together in one group, they give about ten gigabytes.

13
00:01:06,722 --> 00:01:10,644
Later, logical volumes will be carved out of that pool.

14
00:01:19,114 --> 00:01:22,298
The group needs a name. We will use vgdata.

15
00:01:22,318 --> 00:01:26,544
The lab already prepared vdb and vdc as physical volumes,

16
00:01:26,564 --> 00:01:28,630
just like in the last video.

17
00:01:36,150 --> 00:01:41,418
First, pvs. Both disks are physical volumes, and the VG column is empty:

18
00:01:41,438 --> 00:01:47,513
neither belongs to a group. Then vgs, which lists volume groups. It prints nothing:

19
00:01:47,533 --> 00:01:48,909
there are none yet.

20
00:01:58,429 --> 00:02:02,340
Now vgcreate, the name of the new group, and a disk. It says:

21
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:04,532
volume group successfully created.

22
00:02:10,002 --> 00:02:15,118
vgs lists it: the name, one physical volume, no logical volumes yet,

23
00:02:15,138 --> 00:02:18,366
and about five gigabytes of size, all free.

24
00:02:23,836 --> 00:02:30,450
And pvs now shows vgdata in the VG column of vdb. The other disk is still free.

25
00:02:37,970 --> 00:02:42,609
To add more space, vgextend, the group, and the second disk. It says:

26
00:02:42,629 --> 00:02:44,094
successfully extended.

27
00:02:49,564 --> 00:02:55,460
vgs: two physical volumes, and the size doubled to about ten gigabytes.

28
00:02:55,480 --> 00:02:58,376
pvs shows both disks inside vgdata.

29
00:03:08,896 --> 00:03:12,332
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

30
00:03:13,852 --> 00:03:17,566
We reboot. Then vgs shows the group again, with both disks:

31
00:03:17,586 --> 00:03:21,363
the group is stored on the disks themselves, so it survives.

32
00:03:30,883 --> 00:03:34,996
To take a disk out, vgreduce, the group, and the disk. It says:

33
00:03:35,016 --> 00:03:36,701
removed from volume group.

34
00:03:36,721 --> 00:03:40,506
This works only while the disk holds no data of the group.

35
00:03:47,976 --> 00:03:54,550
vgs shows one physical volume and five gigabytes again, and pvs shows vdc free,

36
00:03:54,570 --> 00:03:56,553
with an empty VG column.

37
00:04:06,073 --> 00:04:10,599
Your turn. The disks vdb and vdc are physical volumes.

38
00:04:10,619 --> 00:04:17,082
Create a volume group called examvg with vdb. Add vdc to it. Run the checker.

39
00:04:23,552 --> 00:04:25,303
Try before you open the solution.

40
00:04:30,823 --> 00:04:33,790
Next up: create and delete logical volumes.

41
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:37,162
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
